# Circuit Analysis Tool — Design Spec

## Overview

A web-based circuit analysis tool for students. Solves symbolic circuit problems including dependent sources, nodal analysis, and mesh analysis. Priority is UX — minimal actions, no palette windows, no drag-drop hell. Designed around how students actually sketch circuits on paper.

---

## Scope

Current scope: resistive circuits only (DC, symbolic). Capacitors and inductors excluded for now — can be added in a later phase without breaking existing architecture.

- Lines define topology first. Components populate lines after.
- Every interaction happens on the canvas. No sidebar jumping.
- Variable system is C-like — define first, use after.
- Solver handles the math. Student handles the setup.

---

## Canvas & Grid

- Grid-based canvas.
- All lines and components snap to grid automatically.
- Lines are the primary drawing primitive — everything else attaches to them.

---

## Drawing Interactions

### Lines (Branches)
Lines are drawn in mode 0. Two methods available simultaneously:

Click-drag — hold and drag for long lines. Snaps to grid throughout. Automatically enforces horizontal/vertical, no diagonal accidents. Release to place.

Click-click — click start point, click end point. Snaps to nearest: symbol terminal > node > line endpoint > grid (priority order). For precise short connections.

Both methods coexist — use whichever fits the situation.

Right double-click drag — erase selection for lines/segments specifically. Does not affect components. Separate from general erase (right-click drag).

Mid-draw controls:
- Left arrow — undo last placed segment while still drawing.
- Right arrow — redo reversed segment while still drawing.
- These only apply mid-draw. Global undo is Ctrl+Z.

Mode reset: press 1 to return to neutral/select mode from any key mode. Escape also works as alias.

### Placing Components
- Select component type by keyboard shortcut, then click a line.
- Component snaps onto the line, splitting it at that point.
- Symbol auto-orients to match line direction (horizontal/vertical). No manual rotation.

### Keyboard Shortcuts

| Key | Action | Behavior |
|-----|--------|----------|
| 0 | Line mode | Draw branches on the grid. Click-drag for long lines, click-click for precise connections. |
| 1 | Select / neutral mode | Returns to neutral from any active key mode. Escape also works. |
| R | Resistor | Click a line to place. |
| S | Independent source | Click a line to place (V or I, set in right double-click). |
| D | Dependent source | Click a line to place (type set in right double-click: VCVS, CCCS, VCCS, CCVS). |
| C | Current symbol | Click a branch to place directional arrow. Right-click placed symbol to reverse direction. |
| V | Voltage segment / branch | Drag over area or multiple branches to define a voltage segment (named, usable as variable). Click a single branch for a simple voltage label. Right-click to reverse +/- polarity. |
| G | Ground | Click a node to assign as ground (0V reference). Only one ground allowed — reassigning moves it. |
| L | Loop current | Two modes: (1) Drag over area — auto-detects loop branches within region, applied if exactly one valid loop found. (2) Manual — press L, click each branch in sequence, close by clicking starting branch again. Auto-detect runs first; falls back to manual prompt if topology is ambiguous. |
| N | Node | Click to explicitly define/name a node (for nodal analysis). Drag to define a supernode spanning multiple nodes. Default nodes auto-exist where branches meet, but N lets you explicitly label and control them. |

### Notes
- Right-click on placed C symbol reverses current direction.
- Right-click on placed V segment reverses +/- polarity.

### Erasing
- Right-click drag — draws an erase selection region. Everything inside is deleted.
- No mode switching required.

### Undo / Redo
- Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y.
- Full history of actions.

