## Inspection & Display

### Single Left Click — Peek
- Click any component, node, or branch segment to inspect it.
- Shows name and current value in a small non-blocking tooltip (no popup, no edit mode).
- Dismisses on click elsewhere.
- Works on: symbols, nodes, branch segments, region-defined entities, loop current arrows.

### Named Variable Toggle
- Global toggle button on canvas (or keyboard shortcut TBD).
- When ON: all named variables display their values as inline annotations directly on the canvas, next to their respective component/node/branch.
- When OFF: canvas is clean, no labels shown unless hovered/clicked.
- Useful for reviewing full solved state at a glance without clicking around.

---

## Component Value & Naming

- Right double-click any component — opens inline popup (no separate window).
- Set value (numeric or symbolic expression).
- Set name/label.
- Available pre-defined variables appear as dropdown choices in value field.
- Expressions support arithmetic (e.g. `2 * V_x`, `3 * I_1`).

---

## Variable System

### Defining Variables
Two ways to define a named variable:

1. Place a dependent source (D), right double-click it — set its controlling variable name (e.g. `V_x`). Then click the controlling component to link them explicitly. No name-matching ambiguity.

2. Left-click drag starting from outside any component — region selection. Declare the selected region as a named entity. Options after selection:
   - Name it (registers as a variable accessible elsewhere)
   - Declare type: node voltage, supernode, branch current, voltage segment
   - Calculate/display value at that point
   - Use as controlling variable for dependent sources

### Variable Scoping
- C-like: variable must be defined before it appears as a choice.
- Undefined names never appear in dropdowns — prevents typos silently breaking the solver.
- All variables (node voltages, loop currents, branch currents, dependent source controllers) share one unified pool.

---

## Analysis Methods

### Nodal Analysis
- Nodes are automatically detected where branches meet.
- Supernodes: left-drag to select a region spanning two nodes and a voltage source between them. Name it. Solver applies supernode constraint automatically.
- Node voltages are named variables, accessible as choices.

### Mesh Analysis
- Press a key (TBD) and click inside an enclosed loop — places a loop current arrow symbol.
- Name the loop current (e.g. `I_1`). Registers as a variable.
- Multiple loop currents coexist. Solver builds KVL equations per loop.
- Regular current direction markers (I key) on branches show assumed current direction. Negative result = opposite direction.

### Coexistence
- Both methods can be used on the same circuit simultaneously.
- All variables from both methods feed into one unified equation system.
- Solver detects method per region and applies appropriate constraints.

---

## Source Modes

Each independent source has a mode toggle in its right double-click window:

| Mode | Behavior | Allowed for |
|------|----------|-------------|
| Normal | Active, uses set value | V source, I source |
| Short circuit | Replaced by wire (0V) | V source only |
| Open circuit | Branch removed (0A) | I source only |

Physically nonsensical options are grayed out — current source cannot short, voltage source cannot open.

### Use Cases Unlocked
- Thevenin/Norton: kill sources to find R_th, reactivate for V_th or I_sc
- Superposition: run circuit multiple times with different sources active/killed, combine results manually
- Advanced loop analysis with controlled source deactivation

---

## Dependent Sources

- Four types: VCVS, CCCS, VCCS, CCVS.
- Placed with D key on a line.
- Right double-click: set expression using pre-defined variables from dropdown.
- Controlling component linked by explicit click after naming — unambiguous for solver.

---

## Solver

### Backend
- Modified Nodal Analysis (MNA) for equation generation.
- Sympy for symbolic algebra — no arithmetic errors, handles parametric values.

### Method Priority
1. Nodal analysis (default)
2. Supernode constraints added when detected
3. Mesh equations added for defined loop currents
4. Dependent source constraint equations added last

### Output
- Solved values for all named variables.
- Method(s) used shown to user.
- If unsolvable, clear error message stating why (underdefined, conflicting constraints, etc.)

